Two graphs, $A$ and $B$, are shown below.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
\begin{scope}
\draw[step=1,gray!25,very thin] (0,0) grid (6,6);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(6.5,0) node[right,font=\scriptsize]{L};
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,6.5) node[above,font=\scriptsize]{gal};
\draw[thick,red] (0,0)--(6,6);
\fill (0,0) circle (2.2pt);
\node[font=\small] at (3,-1.3) {$A$: litres to gallons};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[shift={(8,0)}]
\draw[step=1,gray!25,very thin] (0,0) grid (6,6);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(6.5,0) node[right,font=\scriptsize]{$^{\circ}$C};
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,6.5) node[above,font=\scriptsize]{$^{\circ}$F};
\draw[thick,blue] (0,2.13)--(6,5.73);
\fill (0,2.13) circle (2.2pt);
\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.1,2.13) {$32$};
\node[font=\small] at (3,-1.3) {$B$: Celsius to Fahrenheit};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Which graph could be used to convert litres to gallons using direct variation reasoning (that is, $0$ units of one equals $0$ of the other)?

